                "Hamilton Helmer's 7 Powers defines a power as a set of conditions that creates the potential for persistent, significant differential returns. Each power needs both a benefit (it improves your cash flow) and a barrier (competitors cannot or will not copy it). His seven: scale economies, network economies, counter-positioning, switching costs, branding, cornered resource and process power. Operational excellence is not on the list: it is necessary but copyable.",
                "Timing matters. Helmer's Power Progression notes that some powers are mostly available at origination (counter-positioning, cornered resource), others during takeoff (scale economies, network economies, switching costs) and others only in stability (branding, process power). Counter-positioning is the most product-relevant: a newcomer adopts a superior business model that the incumbent does not copy because doing so would damage its existing business, as with index funds against active managers.",
